# Consent > Provide User Consent to the SDK

There are two primary ways to provide or update the user's consent status in the SDK:

- During [Initialization](/docs/data/salesforce-interactions-sdk/guide/c360a-api-initialization.md) for first load
- Using `updateConsents()` for real-time changes

The recommended approach is to provide a JavaScript `Promise` for the `consent` object within the `SalesforceInteractions.init()` configuration. The SDK waits for the `Promise` to resolve before it starts collecting data. The `Promise` must resolve with an array of consent data objects, as shown in this example.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.init({
  consents: new Promise((resolve) => {
    // This function will resolve the Promise when the user takes an action.

    // Example: User clicks the button that grants consent
    document.getElementById("opt-in").addEventListener(
      "click",
      () => {
        resolve([
          {
            provider: "Test Provider",
            purpose: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentPurpose.Tracking,
            status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptIn,
          },
        ]);
      },
      { once: true },
    );

    // Example: User clicks the button that revokes consent
    document.getElementById("opt-out").addEventListener(
      "click",
      () => {
        resolve([
          {
            provider: "Test Provider",
            purpose: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentPurpose.Tracking,
            status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptOut,
          },
        ]);
      },
      { once: true },
    );
  }),
});
```

# Consent > updateConsents()

Use the `updateConsents()` method to update a user's consent status at any time after the initial SDK configuration. For example, when a user changes their consent preferences mid-session.

The syntax for updating consent using this method is `SalesforceInteractions.updateConsents(consents: Consent | Consent[]): void`. The `updateConsents` method accepts either a single `Consent` object or an array of `Consent` objects and does not return a value.

Here's an example of calling the `updateConsents()` method directly from a third-party consent management provider's event handler.

```javascript
// Example: Calling directly from your OneTrust or custom consent management provider's code
// When the user opts in
SalesforceInteractions.updateConsents({
  purpose: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentPurpose.Tracking,
  provider: "OneTrust",
  status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptIn,
});

// When the user opts out and revokes tracking
SalesforceInteractions.updateConsents({
  purpose: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentPurpose.Tracking,
  provider: "OneTrust",
  status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptOut,
});
```

# Data Collection > Data Collection

Before the Salesforce Interactions SDK can begin capturing data, initialize the Salesforce Interactions SDK and gain the user’s consent to track their activity.

If your site has an existing consent management platform, integrate its signals with the SDK's `init` function to confirm the user has granted permission to track.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.init({
  consents: [
    {
      purpose: "Tracking",
      provider: "OneTrust",
      status: "Opt In",
    },
  ],
});
```

For more information, see [Initialization](/docs/data/salesforce-interactions-sdk/guide/c360a-api-initialization.md) and [Consent](/docs/data/salesforce-interactions-sdk/guide/c360a-api-consent.md)

# Data Collection > Capture User Engagement Data

Once initialization is complete, you can track a user's actions by sending an interaction object within the `sendEvent()` function's payload. The `name` field of the interaction specifies the type of action that occurred.

This is the foundation for understanding user behavior, tracking conversions, and personalizing the user experience.

```javascript
// Example: Tracking when a user views a specific product.
SalesforceInteractions.sendEvent({
  interaction: {
    name: "View Catalog Object",
    catalogObject: {
      type: "Product",
      id: "65e4e737",
      attributes: {
        description: "Classic black running shoes",
      },
    },
  },
});
```

# Data Collection > Capture Profile Data

To identify a user or update their profile attributes, send a `user` object within the `sendEvent()` function's payload. This is essential for building a unified profile of your customer across different sessions and devices.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.sendEvent({
  user: {
    attributes: {
      email: "user@domain.com",
    },
  },
});
```

# Data Collection > Combine Profile and Engagement Data

When capturing profile data, you aren’t limited to that minimal interaction format. You can combine any of the interaction data models with profile information to collect as much context for an event as possible.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.sendEvent({
  interaction: {
    name: "View Catalog Object",
    catalogObject: {
      type: "Product",
      id: "65e4e737",
      attributes: {
        description: "Classic black running shoes",
      },
    },
  },
  user: {
    attributes: {
      email: "user@domain.com",
    },
  },
});
```

