# Identity Management > Sharing a Cookie Across Multiple Domains

If you must share cookies across multiple domains, configure the Salesforce Interactions Web SDK with the shared domain name. This can be done through either configuration passed to `SalesforceInteractions.init` or in a function call to `SalesforceInteractions.setCookieDomain`.

For example, to share a cookie between the domains `abc.domain.com` and `xyz.domain.com`, configure the cookie domain with:

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.init({
  cookieDomain: "domain.com",
});
```

You can also set the cookie domain using the `setCookieDomain` function.

# Identity Management > getCookieDomain()

Returns the value of the cookie domain currently used to generate the first-party cookie. The first party cookie stores the `anonymousId`.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.getCookieDomain();
// => "domain.com"
```

# Identity Management > getAnonymousId()

Returns the value of the `anonymousId` persisted in the first-party cookie.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.getAnonymousId();
// => "efc9953d6515dc7f"
```

# Identity Management > setAnonymousId(newAnonymousId:string): void

Manually sets the value of the `anonymousId` persisted in the first-party cookie.

```javascript
// Example: Setting a new ID based on a time stamp
SalesforceInteractions.setAnonymousId(`id_${Date.now()}`);
```

# Initialization > Initialization

The initialization process sets up the core Interactions SDK, configuring its identity management, consent status, and loading the sitemap. This process must complete successfully before the SDK can begin collecting data.

The recommended flow follows a general pattern: first, initialize the SDK with necessary configurations (using `init()`), and then, load the sitemap (using `initSitemap()`).

:::note
**Critical**: Please ensure that you have obtained the appropriate consumer consent required before initializing the Salesforce Interactions Web SDK (Data 360). If you have questions regarding what consent is required, please contact your legal representatives. Salesforce can only process consumer consent choices that have been entered by the Customer.
:::

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.init({
  consents: [...],
  cookieDomain: '...'
}).then(() => {
  SalesforceInteractions.initSitemap({
    global: { ... },
    pageTypeDefault: { ... },
    pageTypes: [...]
  })
})
```

# Initialization > init() (Part 2)

  - : Use a `Promise` when waiting for a user action (like clicking an opt-in button) or for a third-party Consent Management Platform (CMP) to load.

    ```javascript
    // Initialize with consent provided by a user interaction
    SalesforceInteractions.init({
      cookieDomain: "domain.com",
      consents: new Promise((resolve) => {
        // user clicks button that grants consent
        document.getElementById("opt-in").addEventListener(
          "click",
          () => {
            resolve([
              {
                provider: "Test Provider",
                purpose: "Tracking",
                status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptIn,
              },
            ]);
          },
          { once: true },
        );

        // User clicks button that revokes consent
        document.getElementById("opt-out").addEventListener(
          "click",
          () => {
            resolve([
              {
                provider: "Test Provider",
                purpose: "Tracking",
                status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptOut,
              },
            ]);
          },
          { once: true },
        );
      }),
    });
    ```

- Synchronously with Pre-Configured Consent

  - : If consent is available immediately (for example, when consent is loaded from a session cookie), you can pass the array directly.

# Initialization > init() (Part 3)

    ```javascript
    // Initialize with immediate, pre-configured Opt In status
    SalesforceInteractions.init({
      cookieDomain: "domain.com",
      consents: [
        {
          provider: "Test Provider",
          purpose: "Tracking",
          status: SalesforceInteractions.ConsentStatus.OptIn,
        },
      ],
    });
    ```

- Initializing with No Consent

  - : If the user has not made a decision, pass an empty array. The SDK will not track until consent is updated later via `updateConsents()`.

    ```javascript
    // Initialize with tracking disabled until consent is provided later
    SalesforceInteractions.init({
      cookieDomain: "domain.com",
      consents: [],
    });
    ```

# Initialization > reinit()

Use the `reinit()` method forces the SDK to reinitialize its state and re-run sitemap evaluation. This is useful for Single Page Applications (SPAs) where the content and URL change without a full page reload. Calling `reinit()` ensures the sitemap is re-evaluated to match the new virtual page.

```nolang
reinit(): void
```

Here's an example of using the `reinit()` method to reinitialize the SDK.

```javascript
SalesforceInteractions.init({...}).then(() => {
  let currentUrl = window.location.href

  // Monitor the URL for changes (e.g., using a framework hook or interval)
   setInterval(() => {
    if (currentUrl !== window.location.href) {
      currentUrl = window.location.href
       // Reinitialize the SDK when the virtual page URL changes
       SalesforceInteractions.reinit()
     }
   }, 200)

  SalesforceInteractions.initSitemap({...})
})
```

# Integrations > OnEventSend

`OnEventSend` is dispatched after an event has been successfully processed, and a request is made to the Event API (or consuming modules). This event contains the `actionEvent` payload processed in the event pipeline.

```javascript
detail: {
  actionEvent: ActionEvent;
}
```

# Integrations > OnPageMatchStatusUpdated

`OnPageMatchStatusUpdated` is dispatched whenever the sitemap evaluates and updates the match status of any page type defined in your sitemap configuration. Use this custom event to debug the sitemap's logic, as it shows the chronological sequence and outcome of the matching process of all page types.

The data provided in this event's `detail` is a `matchStatus` array that accumulates the status of every page type checked so far.

```javascript
detail: {
    matchStatus: PageMatchStatus[]
}
```

The `PageMatchStatus` object has this structure.

```javascript
{
  pageName: string;
  status: MatchStatus;
  startTime?: number;
  endTime?: number;
  _reject?: any;
}
```

A page type with a status of `running` includes the `startTime` of the evaluation. Conversely, when a page type evaluation concludes with a status of `matched`, `rejected`, or `selected`, the output includes the `endTime`.

Critically, the very first page type to successfully match the current URL is given the status `selected`. After a page type is selected, the sitemap stops evaluating any other remaining page types.

# Integrations > OnException

This event is dispatched when a critical operation within the SDK fails, providing error information and context. Use this custom event for error logging and monitoring runtime errors.

```javascript
detail: {
    error: Error,
    context: string
}
```

The `Error` object has this structure.

```javascript
{
  stack?: string;
}
```

# Integrations > OnSetAnonymousId

`OnSetAnonymousId` is dispatched every time the Web SDK assigns a new tracking identity (`anonymousId`) to the customer's device or browser, and contains this `detail`.

```javascript
{
  newAnonymousId: string;
}
```

# Integrations > Listen for SDK Custom Events

You use the standard browser `document.addEventListener()` method to attach a function to the event's string name (prefixed with `salesforce:`), as shown in this example.

```javascript
document.addEventListener("salesforce:onEventSend", (customEvent) => {
  console.log("On Event Send: ", customEvent.detail);
});

SalesforceInteractions.sendEvent({
  interaction: {
    name: "integration_hook_test",
  },
});
```

