# Configure Amazon S3 Bucket Policies and Permissions > Restrict Access to a VPC Endpoint (Part 2)

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowDataCloudIngestion",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:user/data-cloud-user"
      },
      "Action": ["s3:GetBucketLocation", "s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name",
        "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

2. Add a `Deny` statement that restricts access to a specific VPC endpoint. In this example, the user named `user-name-1` manages the bucket. The Principal element can't accommodate IAM groups, but you can add your bucket administrator on a user-by-user basis.

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "Policy55",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "Access-to-specific-VPCE-only",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::cdpdemo123",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:iam::123456789111:user/user-name-1",
          "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-7e4ef417"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

# Configure Amazon S3 Bucket Policies and Permissions > Add a VPC Endpoint Restriction to Your Bucket Policy

1. Add a `Deny` statement with an `aws:SourceVpce` condition that references your VPC endpoint ID. Adapt the Principal, Resource, and Condition values to coexist with your existing bucket policies and other workloads.

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyAccessOutsideVPCEndpoint",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:user/data-cloud-user"
      },
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name",
        "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-0123456789abcdef0"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

2. Replace `vpce-0123456789abcdef0` with your VPC endpoint ID. You can find this ID in the AWS VPC console under **Endpoints**.

:::note
This statement denies any S3 request from the Data 360 IAM user that doesn't originate from your VPC endpoint. You grant the IAM user S3 access separately, either through an identity-based IAM policy or a resource-based `Allow` statement in the bucket policy.
:::

# Configure Amazon S3 Bucket Policies and Permissions > Configure Your KMS Key Policy

1. Grant decrypt permissions to the Data 360 IAM user, scoped to requests made through the S3 service using the `kms:ViaService` condition. This condition restricts the KMS key so that only S3 in your region can use it.

:::note
Don't add VPC endpoint conditions to the KMS key policy.
:::

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowS3ServiceDecrypt",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:user/data-cloud-user"
      },
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "kms:ViaService": "s3.REGION.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

2. Replace `REGION` with your S3 bucket's AWS region (for example, `us-east-1`).

:::note
In a KMS key policy, `*` refers to the key policy is attached to. In an IAM identity-based policy, you specify the KMS key ARN instead of `*`.
:::

